# The artifact bundle IS committed, deliberately.
#
# It is a build output, and the instinct is to keep build outputs out of git. But the
# deployment target builds from the committed repository, so anything not tracked here is
# simply absent from the build context -- and the image bakes the bundle in rather than
# mounting it, precisely so the image tag fully describes what the service predicts.
#
# The alternatives are worse. Training during the image build would put a ~2 GB job on a
# 2 GB host at deploy time. Mounting the bundle at runtime would decouple the image tag
# from the behaviour and reintroduce the "which model is actually serving?" question.
#
# So the 4.8 MB is tracked. Re-run `make train` and commit the result after any change
# that affects features, preprocessing, or models -- `make verify-bundle` checks that the
# committed bundle matches the committed code.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editors
.idea/
.vscode/

# Streamlit
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# Local environment
.env

# Credentials and deploy keys. Never tracked: a `git add -A` will otherwise sweep a
# private key into a commit, and rewriting history is the only way back out.
.ssh/
*.pem
*_rsa
*_ed25519
id_*
!*.pub.example
